\begin{titlepage}
\centering
\vspace*{2.2cm}
{\fontsize{46}{50}\selectfont\bfseries\color{vhead} vesti}\\[6pt]
{\Large\color{vink} A transpiler that compiles into \LaTeX}\\[26pt]
{\LARGE\bfseries\color{vhead} The Full Reference Manual}\\[10pt]
{\large\color{vink} Language grammar, keywords, builtins, the preprocessor,\\
and the embedded Lua scripting API}\\[34pt]

\begin{tcolorbox}[width=0.74\textwidth, colback=vbg, colframe=vrule, boxrule=0.6pt, arc=3pt]
\centering\ttfamily\small\color{vink}
docclass article (10pt)\\
importpkg \{ amsmath, geometry (a4paper) \}\\[2pt]
startdoc\\
Hello, \textbf{Vesti}!
\end{tcolorbox}

\vfill
{\small\color{vink}
This manual is generated from a full reading of the vesti source tree\\
(lexer, parser, preprocessor, code generator and the Lua bindings).\\
It documents every keyword, builtin, preprocessor directive and Lua API\\
function exposed by the compiler.}\\[10pt]
{\small\color{vhead}\textbf{Status:} beta --- breaking changes may still occur.}
\end{titlepage}

\tableofcontents
\thispagestyle{fancy}
\clearpage
-%

\section{Introduction}
Vesti is a transpiler: it reads a {\tt.ves} source file and emits \LaTeX, which
a \TeX\ engine then turns into a PDF. The motivation is that raw \LaTeX\ is
verbose --- especially the endless \verb|\begin{...}| / \verb|\end{...}| pairs
--- while its markup is uncomfortable to write. Vesti keeps the full power of
\LaTeX\ (you may drop into raw \LaTeX\ at any point) and adds a small, readable
layer of keywords, math shorthands, a macro preprocessor and an embedded Lua
scripting engine.

The \LaTeX\ on the left is produced by the vesti on the right:

\begin{minipage}[t]{0.49\textwidth}
%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=tex]
\documentclass[tikz,geometry]{coprime}
\begin{document}
\section{Foo}
Hello, World!
\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \draw (0,0) -- (1,1);
  \end{tikzpicture}
\end{figure}
\end{document}
\end{lstlisting}
-%
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.49\textwidth}
%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
docclass coprime (tikz, geometry)

startdoc
\section{Foo}
Hello, World!
useenv figure [ht] {
  \centering
  useenv tikzpicture {
    \draw (0,0) -- (1,1);
  }
}
\end{lstlisting}
-%
\end{minipage}

\noindent Notice there is no closing \keyword{%-startdoc-%}: vesti ends the document
automatically at end-of-file, since virtually no real document has material
after \verb|\end{document}|.

\paragraph{Conventions.} Throughout this manual, \keyword{%-keywords-%} are shown in
purple, \builtin|builtins| in blue (always prefixed with \verb|#|), and the
symbol \ob\ marks a position where a space is mandatory. Code samples are set in
framed verbatim blocks.

\section{Installation and configuration}
\subsection{Building the compiler}
Vesti is written in Zig (master branch) with an optional Rust-based Tectonic
backend. For an ordinary install with Tectonic bundled in:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=sh]
$ zig build --prefix-exe-dir <install-path> -Doptimize=ReleaseSafe
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent To build without the Tectonic backend (you then drive an external
\TeX\ engine yourself), add \verb|-Dtectonic=false|. Building the Tectonic
dynamic library additionally needs a Rust toolchain and the native libraries
{\tt fontconfig}, {\tt freetype2}, {\tt graphite2}, {\tt harfbuzz}, {\tt ICU4C}
and {\tt libpng}, plus the {\tt upx} binary. On Linux the {\tt zenity} dialog
tool is also recommended.

\subsection{The configuration file}
Vesti reads an optional configuration file written in {\it Zig Object Notation}
({\tt.zon}). Its location is \verb|~/.config/vesti/config.zon| on Linux and
macOS, or \verb|%APPDATA%\vesti\config.zon| on Windows. All fields are optional;
the example below shows every field at its default value.

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
.{
    .engine = .tectonic,        // default LaTeX engine
    .lua = .{
        .make_log = false,      // dump failing Lua to .vesti-dummy/luacode.lua
        .line_limit = 45,       // above this many lines, log instead of print
    },
}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{\LaTeX\ engines}
Vesti can target five engines. The engine may be chosen (in increasing order of
precedence) by the config file, a command-line flag, the \builtin|engine_type|
builtin, or the {\tt engine} field of {\tt vesti.compile} in {\tt first.lua}.

useenv table [ht] {
    \centering
    useenv tabular (lll) {
        \toprule
        \bf Engine & \bf CLI flag & \bf string id \\
        \midrule
        {\tt latex}    & \verb|--!latex / -L|    & {\tt "plain"} or {\tt "latex"} \\
        {\tt pdflatex} & \verb|--!pdflatex / -p| & {\tt "pdf"} \\
        {\tt xelatex}  & \verb|--!xelatex / -x|  & {\tt "xe"} \\
        {\tt lualatex} & \verb|--!lualatex / -l| & {\tt "lua"} \\
        {\tt tectonic} & \verb|--!tectonic / -T| & {\tt "tect"} \\
        \bottomrule
    }
    \caption{\LaTeX\ engines and how to select them}
}

\noindent The {\it string id} column gives the values accepted by
\builtin|engine_type| and by {\tt vesti.compile\{ engine = ... \}}. Note that
\builtin|engine_type| additionally accepts {\tt "plain"} for the bare
{\tt latex} engine, whereas {\tt vesti.compile} uses {\tt "latex"}.

\section{The command-line interface}
The {\tt vesti} executable exposes the sub-commands below.

\subsection{{\tt compile} --- transpile and build}
useenv center {
    {\tt vesti compile}\ob[\verb|<FILENAME>|]?\ob[\verb|<flags>|]?
}
If \verb|<FILENAME>| is omitted, vesti looks for a {\tt first.lua} build script.
The accepted flags are:

useenv table [ht] {
    \centering
    useenv tabular (ll) {
        \toprule
        \bf Flag & \bf Meaning \\
        \midrule
        \verb|--!emit_tex, -e|    & emit the {\tt.tex} only (reserved; currently a no-op) \\
        \verb|--!exit_err, -E|    & in watch mode, exit immediately on the first error \\
        \verb|--!no_color, -N|    & disable coloured terminal output \\
        \verb|--!standalone, -S|  & compile without assuming a {\tt first.lua} exists \\
        \verb|--!watch, -W|       & rebuild when source files change \\
        \verb|--!latex, -L|       & use the {\tt latex} engine \\
        \verb|--!pdflatex, -p|    & use {\tt pdflatex} \\
        \verb|--!xelatex, -x|     & use {\tt xelatex} \\
        \verb|--!lualatex, -l|    & use {\tt lualatex} \\
        \verb|--!tectonic, -T|    & use {\tt tectonic} \\
        \verb|--!lim N|           & number of compile cycles (default {\tt 3}) \\
        \verb|--!first_script F|  & build-once Lua script (default {\tt first.lua}) \\
        \verb|--!before_script F| & Lua run before each pass (default {\tt before.lua}) \\
        \verb|--!step_script F|   & Lua run at each step end (default {\tt step.lua}) \\
        \bottomrule
    }
    \caption{Flags of {\tt vesti compile}}
}

\subsection{{\tt latex} --- build an existing {\tt.tex} with Tectonic}
\verb|vesti latex <FILENAME>| compiles a plain \LaTeX\ file through the bundled
Tectonic engine. It accepts \verb|--!no_color|, \verb|--!watch|, \verb|--!lim| and
the three script flags above.

\subsection{{\tt init} --- scaffold a project}
\verb|vesti init <PROJECT>| creates \verb|<PROJECT>.ves| (a tiny ``Hello, World!''
document) together with a {\tt first.lua} build script wired to
{\tt vesti.compile}.

\subsection{{\tt clear} --- remove build artefacts}
\verb|vesti clear| deletes the {\tt.vesti-dummy} working directory.

\subsection{{\tt experimental}}
\verb|vesti experimental <FILENAME>| is a sandbox for in-development features and
is not intended for production use.

\section{Project structure and the build pipeline}
A vesti build is orchestrated by up to three Lua scripts and writes its
intermediate files into a hidden working directory, {\tt.vesti-dummy}.

useenv table [ht] {
    \centering
    useenv tabular (ll) {
        \toprule
        \bf File & \bf Role \\
        \midrule
        {\tt first.lua}    & Build entry point, run {\it once}. The only place {\tt vesti.compile} may be called. \\
        {\tt before.lua}   & Run once {\it before each} compilation pass (e.g.\ to download fonts). \\
        {\tt step.lua}     & Run at the {\it end of each} compile step (post-processing hooks). \\
        {\tt.vesti-dummy}  & Working directory holding generated {\tt.tex}, copied assets and downloads. \\
        \bottomrule
    }
    \caption{The files that drive a vesti build}
}

\noindent A freshly initialised {\tt first.lua} looks like:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=luaved]
-- below code imports a vesti module
-- vesti.getModule("module_name")
vesti.compile("myproject.ves", { engine = "tect", compile_all = true })
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent With the \verb|-S| / \verb|--!standalone| flag vesti skips the
{\tt first.lua} search and compiles the named file directly. Because compilation
happens relative to {\tt.vesti-dummy}, asset paths must be rewritten with
\builtin|get_filepath| or \builtin|copy_file| rather than passed raw to
\verb|\includegraphics| and friends.

\section{Structure of a vesti file}
Like \LaTeX, a vesti document has two regions: the {\bf preamble} and the {\bf
main body}, separated by \keyword{%-startdoc-%}.

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
docclass article (10pt)        -- preamble: class
importpkg {                    -- preamble: packages
    geometry (a4paper, margin = 2.2cm),
}
startdoc                       -- everything after is the body
Hello, Vesti!
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent In the preamble the keywords \keyword{%-docclass-%} and \keyword{%-importpkg-%}
carry their special meaning; once \keyword{%-startdoc-%} is seen they become ordinary
text, and a second \keyword{%-startdoc-%} in the body does nothing. There is no
closing keyword --- end-of-file terminates the document.

\section{Lexical grammar}
This section describes how raw characters become tokens.

\subsection{Comments}
Vesti comments are {\it Lua-style}, not \LaTeX-style. A line comment begins with
two hyphens and runs to the end of the line; block comments use long-bracket
syntax with an optional level of {\tt=} signs:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
Hello   -- a vesti line comment, removed before output
--[[ a block comment
     spanning several lines ]]
--[==[ contains ]] and ]=] safely ]==]
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent Because two hyphens start a comment, the dashes you may want in prose
have dedicated spellings: write {\tt -}{\tt -}{\tt !}\ for an en-dash and {\tt
-}{\tt -}{\tt -}\ for an em-dash. A percent sign {\tt\%}\ is {\it not} a vesti comment: it is
passed through as a \LaTeX\ comment.

\subsection{Escapes and literal characters}
Some characters are meaningful to vesti and must be escaped to appear verbatim
in the output:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
  \#    ->  #     literal sharp (otherwise begins a builtin)
  \$    ->  $     literal dollar (otherwise a math switch)
  $#    ->  $     alternative literal dollar
  \%    ->  %     literal percent sign
  #!    ->  #     literal sharp before an identifier (e.g. Lua's #t)
  \{ \} ->  { }   math braces
  \[ \] ->        display-math delimiters
  \,    ->        thin math space
  \;    ->        wide math space
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{Raw \LaTeX\ pass-through (verbatim)}
Two constructs hand bytes straight to \LaTeX, untouched by vesti's lexer. A
{\bf block} of raw \LaTeX\ is written between {\tt\%-} and {\tt-\%}; a single
{\bf line} of raw \LaTeX\ follows {\tt\%\#}. For instance, the single-line form is
used for a one-off definition such as

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
  \def\thefootnote{(\roman{footnote})}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent and the block form for a longer fragment that vesti should not touch:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
  \begin{tabular}{cc} a & b \\ c & d \end{tabular}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{Numbers, words and \LaTeX\ commands}
Runs of digits form integers, with a decimal point producing a float. Runs of
alphabetic characters form text; a text run equal to a reserved word becomes
that keyword. A backslash followed by letters is a \LaTeX\ command. When
\builtin|at_on| is active the {\tt @} character is also a command letter, and
under \builtin|ltx3_on| so are {\tt\_}\ and {\tt:}\ (expl3 names).

\subsection{Math symbol conversion}
Inside math mode many ASCII sequences are rewritten into the corresponding
\LaTeX\ commands; outside math mode they stay ordinary text. The complete table
baked into the lexer:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
  <=    ->  \leq                 ->    ->  \rightarrow
  >=    ->  \geq                 <-    ->  \leftarrow
  !=    ->  \neq                 <->   ->  \leftrightarrow
  /=    ->  \neq                 =>    ->  \Rightarrow
  -->   ->  \longrightarrow      <--   ->  \longleftarrow
  ==>   ->  \Longrightarrow      <==   ->  \Longleftarrow
  <-->  ->  \longleftrightarrow  <==>  ->  \Longleftrightarrow
  <=>   ->  \Leftrightarrow      |->   ->  \mapsto
  -!    ->  \setminus            {<    ->  \langle
  >}    ->  \rangle              ||    ->  \|
  ...   ->  \cdots               oo    ->  \infty
  //    ->  \frac{..}{..}  (a fraction; see the Math mode section)
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent The {\tt oo} sequence only becomes \(\infty\) when it stands alone;
{\tt food} keeps its letters, because the lexer checks that no alphabetic
character follows.

\section{Keywords}
The following words are reserved; they act as keywords only where they are
syntactically valid (for example \keyword{%-docclass-%} only in the preamble) and
otherwise fall back to ordinary text. The table below is generated directly from
{\tt ../src/lexer/Token.zig} at compile time, so it always reflects the current
{\tt VESTI\_KEYWORDS} set.

useenv table [ht] {
    \centering
    #::
    local content = read_all("../src/lexer/Token.zig")
    -- match `.{ "name", TokenType.X }`, dropping the deprecated entries.
    local pat = "%.%{%s*\"([^\"]+)\"%s*,%s*TokenType%.([%w_]+)"
    local keywords = ves_collect(content, pat, "deprecated")
    ves_name_table(keywords, 4, "\\keyword{%s}")
    ::#
    \caption{Keywords in Vesti (auto-generated from the source)}
}

\subsection{\keyword{%-docclass-%} --- document class}
The analogue of \verb|\documentclass|; active only in the preamble.
useenv center {
    \keyword{%-docclass-%}\ob\verb|<class-name>|\ob{\tt(}\verb|<options>|{\tt)}
}
Options are comma-separated and parenthesised. Examples: \keyword{%-docclass-%}\ob{\tt
article}, \keyword{%-docclass-%}\ob{\tt article\ob(10pt)}, and \keyword{%-docclass-%}\ob{\tt
article\ob(10pt,\ob twocols)}.

\subsection{\keyword{%-importpkg-%} --- load packages}
The analogue of \verb|\usepackage|, also preamble-only. It has two forms. The
single-package form mirrors \keyword{%-docclass-%}; the braced form loads several
packages, each with its own options:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
importpkg {
    amsmath, amssymb, amsthm,
    geometry (a4paper, margin = 2.2cm),
    xcolor (dvipsnames),
}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{\keyword{%-importves-%} --- include another vesti file}
\keyword{%-importves-%}{\tt(}\verb|<path.ves>|{\tt)}\ resolves the path, name-mangles
it, and emits \verb|\input{...}| of the corresponding compiled file. This lets a
project be split across many {\tt.ves} sources.

\subsection{\keyword{%-importmod-%} --- fetch and use a vesti module}
\keyword{%-importmod-%}{\tt(}\verb|<module-name>|{\tt)}\ downloads (if necessary) and
installs an external vesti module. The Lua equivalent is {\tt vesti.getModule}.

\subsection{\keyword{%-startdoc-%} --- begin the body}
Marks the end of the preamble and the start of the main body (it expands to
\verb|\begin{document}|). A second \keyword{%-startdoc-%} inside the body is a no-op.

\subsection{\keyword{%-useenv-%} --- an environment}
\keyword{%-useenv-%} replaces a \verb|\begin{...}| / \verb|\end{...}| pair with a
single brace-delimited block; vesti tracks the nesting and emits the matching
end for you.
useenv center {
    \keyword{%-useenv-%}\ob\verb|<env-name>|\ob\verb|<arg>|*\ob{\tt\{}\ \verb|<body>|\ {\tt\}}
}
Each \verb|<arg>| is either {\tt(}{\it mandatory}{\tt)} or {\tt[}{\it
optional}{\tt]}; there may be zero or more, and {\bf no spaces may separate
consecutive arguments}.

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
useenv figure [ht] {
    \centering
    useenv tikzpicture {
        \draw (0,0) -- (1,1);
    }
}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{\keyword{%-begenv-%} / \keyword{%-endenv-%} --- explicit pair}
These are the explicit, unmatched analogues of \verb|\begin| and \verb|\end|.
The block \keyword{%-begenv-%}\ob{\tt center} ... \keyword{%-endenv-%}\ is equivalent to
\keyword{%-useenv-%}\ob{\tt center \{ ... \}}. Since \keyword{%-useenv-%} already
auto-matches, why keep them? {\it Defining new environments}: the body of a
\keyword{%-defenv-%} needs an unmatched begin in one half and an unmatched end in the
other. Two modifiers may follow the keyword with no space: a {\tt!} gives the
{\bf phantom} form (do not push/pop the matching stack), and {\tt*} emits a
trailing newline after the begin.

\subsection{\keyword{%-defun-%} --- define a command}
\keyword{%-defun-%} defines a \LaTeX\ command. With a parameter specification it uses
the modern {\tt xparse} interface; without one it falls back to the primitive
\verb|\def| family.
useenv center {
    \keyword{%-defun-%}\ob[\verb|<attr>|]?\ob\verb|<name>|\ob(\verb|<spec>|)?\ob{\tt\{}\verb|<body>|{\tt\}}
}
Examples:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
defun foo {Hello, World!}
defun [!] keyword (m) {{\tt\color{vkw}\bfseries #1}}
defun [r] Re {#mathmode{\mathop{"Re"}}}
defun defeq {\coloneq}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

The optional {\tt[}\verb|<attr>|{\tt]}\ bracket holds single-character flags that
select how the command is defined and how the body's surrounding whitespace is
trimmed:

useenv table [ht] {
    \centering
    useenv tabular (cl) {
        \toprule
        \bf Flag & \bf Effect \\
        \midrule
        {\tt r} / {\tt R} & redefine: do not error if the name exists \\
        {\tt !}           & declare: use the non-\verb|\protected| primitive / \verb|\DeclareDocumentCommand| \\
        {\tt e} / {\tt E} & expand: use the \verb|\edef| family / expandable xparse \\
        {\tt g} / {\tt G} & global: use the \verb|\gdef| / \verb|\xdef| family \\
        {\tt p} / {\tt P} & provide (xparse only): \verb|\ProvideDocumentCommand| \\
        {\tt \textless} / {\tt \textgreater} & do not trim whitespace on the left / right of the body \\
        \bottomrule
    }
    \caption{Attributes of \keyword{%-defun-%}}
}

In the primitive form the body references arguments with the parameter tokens
\builtin|1| through \builtin|9| (becoming {\tt\#1}..{\tt\#9}); doubled nesting is
written by prefixing the index with the nesting level, so \builtin|11| becomes
{\tt\#\#1}\ and \builtin|21| becomes {\tt\#\#\#\#1}. In xparse mode the
\verb|<spec>| is an {\tt xparse} argument string such as {\tt m}, {\tt o} or
{\tt v}. To define a command whose name contains {\tt @}, wrap the definition in
\builtin|at_on| / \builtin|at_off|.

\subsection{\keyword{%-defenv-%} --- define an environment}
\keyword{%-defenv-%} defines a new environment; it always uses the xparse
\verb|\NewDocumentEnvironment| family. The body is given as {\it two} braced
blocks --- the begin code and the end code:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
defenv foo {begenv! minipage(\textwidth)}{endenv!}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent Attributes: {\tt r}/{\tt R} (redefine), {\tt p}/{\tt P} (provide),
{\tt!}\ (declare), {\tt\textless}/{\tt\textgreater}\ keep the begin block's left/right whitespace,
and {\tt(}/{\tt)}\ keep the end block's left/right whitespace.

\section{Math mode}
Vesti tracks whether it is in {\bf text mode} or {\bf math mode}. The symbol
conversions above and the constructs below apply only in math mode.

\subsection{Entering math mode}
Inline math is delimited by {\tt\$}\ldots{\tt\$}; display math by
{\tt\$\$}\ldots{\tt\$\$}\ or by \verb|\[|\ldots\verb|\]|. A math block must be
properly closed; an unbalanced switch is a parse error.

\subsection{Fractions with {\tt//}}
Inside a brace group in math mode, the {\tt//}\ operator splits the group into a
numerator and a denominator and emits \verb|\frac|:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
$$ {1 // n^2} $$            -- becomes  \frac{1}{n^2}
$$ {\pi^2 // 6} $$          -- becomes  \frac{\pi^2}{6}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{Auto-sized delimiters with {\tt?}}
A {\tt?}\ immediately {\it before} an opening delimiter, or immediately {\it
after} a closing delimiter, requests an auto-sized (\verb|\left|/\verb|\right|)
delimiter. The recognised delimiters are:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
( )    [ ]    {< >}    \{ \}    |    ||    .
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent (a lone dot yields an invisible delimiter).

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=sh]
$ ?( \frac{a}{b} )? $       -- \left( \frac{a}{b} \right)
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{Text inside math with {\tt"\ldots"}}
Double quotes drop back into text mode for their contents (like \verb|\text{}|).
Prefixing or suffixing the quotes with a raw sharp {\tt\#}\ adds a surrounding
space:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=sh]
$ x = "if " n " is even" $  -- text spans, no auto spaces
$ a #"and"# b $             -- a space before and after the word
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{Mode-switching builtins}
\builtin|mathmode| forces math interpretation of its braced body (and errors if
already in math mode); \builtin|textmode| does the reverse. They are
indispensable when a command body must be parsed in a particular mode regardless
of where the command is later used.

\section{Builtins}
Builtins are compiler intrinsics, written with a leading {\tt\#}. The prefix
exists so that common English words (like {\it label}) are not stolen from
ordinary prose. The {\bf evaluated} builtins below are generated from {\tt
VESTI\_BUILTINS} in {\tt ../src/lexer/Token.zig}:

useenv table [ht] {
    \centering
    #::
    local content = read_all("../src/lexer/Token.zig")
    -- isolate the VESTI_BUILTINS map, then list its single-string entries.
    local block = content:match("VESTI_BUILTINS.-initComptime(%b())")
    local pat = "%.%{%s*\"([^\"]+)\"%s*%}"
    local builtins = ves_collect(block, pat, nil)
    ves_name_table(builtins, 5, "\\builtin@%s@")
    ::#
    \caption{Evaluated builtins (auto-generated from the source)}
}

\noindent The {\bf preprocessor} builtins (resolved in an earlier pass; see the
next section) come from {\tt VESTI\_PREPROCESS\_BUILTINS} in the same file:

useenv table [ht] {
    \centering
    #::
    local content = read_all("../src/lexer/Token.zig")
    local block = content:match("VESTI_PREPROCESS_BUILTINS.-initComptime(%b())")
    local pat = "%.%{%s*\"([^\"]+)\"%s*%}"
    local builtins = ves_collect(block, pat, nil)
    ves_name_table(builtins, 4, "\\builtin@%s@")
    ::#
    \caption{Preprocessor builtins (auto-generated from the source)}
}

Since {\tt fancyvrb} does not allow verbatim-like commands inside the {\tt
Verbatim} environment, builtins are not coloured inside example code blocks.

\subsection{\texttt{\#label}}
\verb|\label| normally has to go {\it inside} an environment body, which reads
awkwardly. \builtin|label| attaches the label to the \keyword{%-useenv-%} that
follows it instead; it must immediately precede a \keyword{%-useenv-%}.

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
#label(eq:1)
useenv theorem {
    1 + 1 = 2.
}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{\texttt{\#eq}}
A shorthand for an {\tt equation} environment (optionally labelled). It must be
used in text mode and switches to math mode for its body:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
#eq(eq:euler) {
    \sum_{n=1}^oo {1 // n^2} = {\pi^2 // 6}.
}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{\texttt{\#chardef}}
\builtin|chardef|\ob\verb|<hex-codepoint>|\ob\verb|<command>|\ defines a text
character token by Unicode code point (hexadecimal), emitting a \verb|\chardef|.
The directive must end at a newline.

\subsection{\texttt{\#mathchardef}}
\builtin|mathchardef|\ob{\tt.}\verb|<class>|\ob\verb|<font-num>|\ob\verb|<hex>|\ob\verb|<command>|
\ defines a math symbol by code point and {\it math class}, emitting a
\verb|\Umathchardef|. The class is one of {\tt.ordinary}\,(0), {\tt.largeop}\,(1),
{\tt.binary}\,(2), {\tt.relation}\,(3), {\tt.opening}\,(4), {\tt.closing}\,(5),
{\tt.punct}\,(6) or {\tt.variable}\,(7).

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
#mathchardef .opening  0 29d8 \lfooo
#mathchardef .ordinary 0 2202 \diff
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{\texttt{\#enum} and \texttt{\#enum\_counter}}
\builtin|enum| is a compact {\tt enumerate} with light {\tt enumitem}-style label
control; it nests up to four deep without {\tt enumitem}. An optional
parenthesised pattern customises the \verb|\item| label: a single {\tt*}\ expands
to the current counter {\tt\{}\verb|<count>|{\tt\}}\ (braces included), while a
doubled {\tt**}\ collapses to one literal {\tt*}.

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
#enum (\Roman*.) {
    \item aaa
    \item #enum ({\roman*}**) {
        \setcounter{#enum_counter}{2}
        \item bbb
    }
}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent Here \verb|\Roman*.| becomes \verb|\Roman{enumi}.| and
\verb|{\roman*}**| becomes \verb|{\roman{enumii}}*|. The companion
\builtin|enum_counter| expands to the {\it name} of the current enum counter,
useful with \verb|\setcounter|.

\subsection{\texttt{\#get\_filepath}}
Because compilation runs inside {\tt.vesti-dummy}, raw relative paths break.
\builtin|get_filepath|{\tt(}\verb|<path>|{\tt)}\ rewrites a path so it is correct
relative to the dummy directory:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
\includegraphics{#get_filepath(./foo.png)}
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent A leading {\tt~}\ expands to the home directory and a leading {\tt @/}\
anchors the path at the vesti config directory.

\subsection{\texttt{\#copy\_file}}
\builtin|copy_file|{\tt(}\verb|<path>|{\tt)}\ copies the named file into {\tt
.vesti-dummy} so the engine can find it. Like \builtin|get_filepath| it
understands the {\tt~}\ and {\tt @/}\ prefixes. It replaces the deprecated
\keyword{%-cpfile-%} keyword.

\subsection{\texttt{\#picture}}
\builtin|picture|\ob[\verb|<unit>|]?\ob({\it w}, {\it h})\ob({\it x}, {\it y})?\ob{\tt\{}\verb|<body>|{\tt\}}\
generates a \LaTeX\ {\tt picture} environment, where {\it w}, {\it h} are integer
width and height, the optional second pair is an offset, and the optional
bracketed argument sets the unit length.

\subsection{\texttt{\#showfont}}
\builtin|showfont|{\tt(}{\it n}{\tt)}\ expands to a
\verb|\ttfamily\meaning\textfont|{\it n} report for math font number {\it n}
(0--!255). A diagnostic aid.

\subsection{\texttt{\#raw\_tex}}
Placed directly before a \keyword{%-defun-%}, \builtin|raw_tex| disables the
automatic xparse machinery so the definition uses the bare \verb|\def| primitive
even when a parameter list is present.

\subsection{\texttt{\#engine\_type}}
\builtin|engine_type|{\tt(}\verb|<id>|{\tt)}\ selects the \LaTeX\ engine from
within the source. It must appear on the very first line and may be used only
once; \verb|<id>| is one of {\tt plain}, {\tt pdf}, {\tt xe}, {\tt lua} or {\tt
tect}. It replaces the deprecated \keyword{%-compty-%} keyword.

\subsection{\texttt{\#textmode} and \texttt{\#mathmode}}
Discussed under Math mode above. \builtin|mathmode|{\tt\{}\verb|<body>|{\tt\}}\
forces math interpretation of its body; \builtin|textmode|{\tt\{}\verb|<body>|{\tt\}}\
forces text interpretation (valid only inside math mode).

\section{The preprocessor}
Before parsing, vesti runs a token-level preprocessing pass that expands
user-defined macros and resolves the eight directives listed in the preprocessor
table above.

\subsection{\texttt{\#def} and \texttt{\#undef} --- token macros}
\builtin|def|\ob\verb|#!name|\ob{\tt\{}\verb|<body>|{\tt\}}\ defines a preprocessor
macro {\tt\#name}. Wherever {\tt\#name} later appears it is replaced by the body,
with expansion applied recursively. Macros may take parameters, referenced in the
body as \builtin|1|, \builtin|2|, ...; arguments are supplied in parentheses at
the call site.

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
#def #greet {Hello, World!}
#def #twice {#1 #1}

#greet                 -- expands to: Hello, World!
#twice(ho)             -- expands to: ho ho
#undef #twice
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent A parameter count is inferred from the highest parameter used in the
body. You cannot \builtin|def| or \builtin|undef| a name that is already a
builtin or keyword, and preprocessor directives may not appear inside a macro
body.

\subsection{\texttt{\#include} --- textual file inclusion}
\builtin|include|{\tt(}\verb|<path>|{\tt)}\ splices the {\it token stream} of
another file into the current one at that point (distinct from \keyword{%-importves-%},
which emits an \verb|\input|). Macros defined in the included file therefore
become visible to the includer. Circular includes are detected and rejected.

\subsection{\texttt{\#at\_on} / \texttt{\#at\_off} --- {\tt @} as a letter}
\builtin|at_on| emits \verb|\makeatletter| {\it and} tells the lexer to treat
{\tt @} as a command-name letter; \builtin|at_off| emits \verb|\makeatother| and
restores the lexer. This is required to \keyword{%-defun-%} commands whose names
contain {\tt @}:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
#at_on
defun @foo (#1) {Hello, #1!}
#at_off
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{\texttt{\#ltx3\_on} / \texttt{\#ltx3\_off} / \texttt{\#noltx3}}
\builtin|ltx3_on| emits \verb|\ExplSyntaxOn| and makes {\tt\_}\ and {\tt:}\
command letters (so you can write expl3 names); \builtin|ltx3_off| emits
\verb|\ExplSyntaxOff|. Since the modern \LaTeX\ kernel permits expl3 without a
separate package, these are enabled by default. The preamble-only \builtin|noltx3|
opts out, forbidding the other two for that file.

\subsection{Defun parameter tokens}
The sharp-number tokens \builtin|1| through \builtin|9| act as parameter
placeholders in both \builtin|def| bodies and \keyword{%-defun-%} bodies. The
two-digit forms encode nesting for definitions-within-definitions: the tens digit
is the nesting level and the units digit the argument index, and the emitted
number of {\tt\#}\ signs doubles with each level.

\section{Lua scripting}
Vesti embeds a full Lua interpreter, used in the build scripts and in inline
blocks. All of Lua's standard library is available, plus a global {\tt vesti}
table of compiler bindings.

\subsection{Inline Lua blocks}
An inline block is delimited by {\tt\#::}\ and {\tt::\#}. The code runs at compile
time; whatever it sends through {\tt vesti.print} (or {\tt vesti.parse}) is
spliced into the document at that position.

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
#::
for i = 1, 3 do
    vesti.print("\\item entry " .. i)
end
::#
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent Note: {\tt vesti.print} inserts its argument as {\it raw} \LaTeX, while
{\tt vesti.parse} first transpiles a string of {\it vesti} into \LaTeX.

\subsection{Block modifiers: global, import, export}
Three optional modifiers may follow the closing {\tt::\#}, in this order:

useenv table [ht] {
    \centering
    useenv tabular (ll) {
        \toprule
        \bf Suffix & \bf Meaning \\
        \midrule
        {\tt ::\#*}        & {\bf global}: definitions persist in the shared Lua state \\
        {\tt ::\#[a, b]}   & {\bf import}: pull the exported chunks {\tt a} and {\tt b} into scope \\
        {\tt ::\#<name\textgreater}   & {\bf export}: register this block's source under {\tt name} \\
        \bottomrule
    }
    \caption{Inline Lua block modifiers}
}

\noindent They compose, e.g.\ {\tt ::\#*[helpers]<more\textgreater}\ runs globally, imports
{\tt helpers}, and exports itself as {\tt more}. A common pattern is to export a
helper block once and import it wherever needed:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
#::
local function read_all(path)
    local f = assert(io.open(path, "rb"))
    local data = f:read("*a"); f:close(); return data
end
::#<readAll>

#::
local content = read_all("data.txt")
vesti.print(content)
::#[readAll]
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\noindent Because the lexer still scans inline blocks, a bare {\tt\#}\ before an
identifier (such as Lua's length operator {\tt\#t}) would be read as a builtin.
Write {\tt\#!t}\ --- the {\tt\#!}\ escape yields a literal {\tt\#}\ --- when you
need {\tt\#}\ immediately before a name.

\subsection{Build scripts}
{\tt first.lua} runs once at the very start and must call {\tt vesti.compile};
{\tt before.lua} runs once before each compilation pass; {\tt step.lua} runs at
the end of each step. A representative {\tt before.lua} that downloads a font:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=luaved]
local dummy = vesti.vestiDummyDir()
local url = "https://example.com/STIXTwoMath-Regular.ttf"
vesti.mkdir(dummy)
vesti.download(url, vesti.joinpath(dummy, "STIXTwoMath-Regular.ttf"))
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\subsection{{\tt vesti.compile} --- the build entry point}
Callable {\it only} from {\tt first.lua}:
\verb|vesti.compile(<main-ves>, { <options> })|. The options table accepts the
string {\tt engine} ({\tt "latex"}, {\tt "pdf"}, {\tt "xe"}, {\tt "lua"} or {\tt
"tect"}) and the booleans {\tt compile\_all}, {\tt watch}, {\tt no\_color} and
{\tt no\_exit\_err}.

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=luaved]
vesti.compile("paper.ves", { engine = "tect", compile_all = true })
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\section{The Lua API reference}
Every binding lives in the global {\tt vesti} table. The list below is generated
from {\tt ../src/Lua.zig} so it always reflects the functions the compiler
actually exposes:

useenv table [ht] {
    \centering
    #::
    local content = read_all("../src/Lua.zig")
    -- every binding appears as `.name = "function"` in the FnReg array.
    local pat = '%.name%s*=%s*"([^"]+)"'
    local lua = ves_collect(content, pat, nil)
    ves_name_table(lua, 4, "{\\tt %s}")
    ::#
    \caption{Lua API functions in Vesti (auto-generated from the source)}
}

\noindent What each function does:

useenv table [ht] {
    \centering
    useenv tabular (ll) {
        \toprule
        \bf Function & \bf Purpose \\
        \midrule
        {\tt print(..., \{sep, nl\})}    & append arguments to the output (raw \LaTeX); {\tt sep} default {\tt" "}, {\tt nl} default {\tt 1}, clamped 0--!2 \\
        {\tt parse(s)}                  & transpile a vesti string to \LaTeX\ and return it \\
        {\tt mkdir(d)}                  & create a directory (silent if it exists) \\
        {\tt joinpath(...)}             & join path components, return the path \\
        {\tt getCurrentDir()}           & return the current working directory \\
        {\tt setCurrentDir(d)}          & change the current working directory \\
        {\tt vestiDummyDir()}           & return the {\tt.vesti-dummy} directory name \\
        {\tt unzip(file, dest)}         & extract a zip into an existing directory \\
        {\tt download(url, file)}       & HTTP GET into a file \\
        {\tt ping(url)}                 & return {\tt true} if the host is reachable \\
        {\tt getModule(name)}           & download and install a vesti module \\
        {\tt getEngineType()}           & return the active engine as a string \\
        {\tt compile(ves, \{opts\})}     & build entry point ({\tt first.lua} only) \\
        \bottomrule
    }
    \caption{Behaviour of the Lua API functions}
}

\section{Deprecated features}
The following keywords have been retired in favour of builtins; using them is a
compile error that names the replacement. This table is generated from the
deprecated entries in {\tt VESTI\_KEYWORDS}:

useenv table [ht] {
    \centering
    #::
    local content = read_all("../src/lexer/Token.zig")
    -- `.{ "name", TokenType.deprecated(bool, "instead") }`
    local pat = "%.%{%s*\"([^\"]+)\"%s*,%s*TokenType%.deprecated%([%w]+,%s*\"([^\"]+)\"%)"
    vesti.print([[\begin{tabular}{ll}]])
    vesti.print([[\toprule]])
    vesti.print([[\bf Deprecated & \bf Replacement \\]])
    vesti.print([[\midrule]])
    for name, instead in content:gmatch(pat) do
        local repl = instead:gsub("^#", "")
        vesti.print(string.format("\\keyword{%s} & \\builtin@%s@ \\\\", name, repl))
    end
    vesti.print([[\bottomrule]])
    vesti.print([[\end{tabular}]])
    ::#
    \caption{Deprecated keywords (auto-generated from the source)}
}

\section{A worked example}
A small but complete document exercising several features at once:

%-
\begin{lstlisting}[style=plainved]
docclass article (11pt)
importpkg {
    amsmath, amssymb,
    geometry (a4paper, margin = 2.5cm),
    graphicx,
}

defun [!] R {\mathbb{R}}              -- a quick math macro
#def #vec {\mathbf{#1}}               -- a parameterised preprocessor macro

startdoc
\section{A demonstration}

The reals are written $#vec(x) in R^n$, and we recall
#eq(eq:zeta) {
    \sum_{n=1}^oo {1 // n^2} = {\pi^2 // 6}.
}

#enum (\Roman*.) {
    \item first
    \item second
}

\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{#get_filepath(./figure.png)}

#::
for _, name in ipairs({ "alpha", "beta", "gamma" }) do
    vesti.print(vesti.parse("\\textbf{" .. name .. "}\\\\"))
end
::#
\end{lstlisting}
-%

\section{Appendix: quick reference}
\paragraph{Auto-generated tables.} The keyword, evaluated-builtin,
preprocessor-builtin, Lua-API and deprecated tables in this manual are produced
by inline Lua that reads {\tt ../src/lexer/Token.zig} and {\tt ../src/Lua.zig}.
Editing the corresponding maps in the compiler source and recompiling this
document updates every list automatically --- no manual edits required.

\paragraph{Syntax at a glance.}
useenv center {
useenv tabular (llll) {
\toprule
{\tt -}{\tt -} & line comment & {\tt -}{\tt -}{\tt [[ ]]} & block comment \\
{\tt -}{\tt -}{\tt !} & en-dash & {\tt -}{\tt -}{\tt -} & em-dash \\
{\tt\%\#} & line verbatim & {\tt\%-}\ldots{\tt-\%} & block verbatim \\
{\tt\$ \ldots \$} & inline math & {\tt\$\$ \ldots \$\$} & display math \\
{\tt \{a // b\}} & fraction (math) & {\tt ?( )?} & sized delimiter \\
{\tt " \ldots "} & text in math & {\tt oo} & infinity (math) \\
{\tt \#1 \ldots \#9} & %-defun-% parameter & {\tt \#!} & literal \# \\
{\tt \#:: \ldots ::\#} & inline Lua & {\tt ::\#*} & global Lua block \\
\bottomrule
}
}

\vskip 1em
\begin{center}\small\itshape
End of the vesti full reference manual.\\
Lists are generated from a live reading of the compiler source.
\end{center}
